const PATTERN_ACADEMY_7 = {
  "dag-dp": {
    title: "Pattern 34 — DAG DP: Topological Sort + Dynamic Programming (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is the final pattern in the Tier 4 graph block. It's especially important because it connects two areas: Graph thinking + Dynamic Programming.",
        conclusion: "The core idea: a DAG gives you a natural order in which to perform DP." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. What is a DAG?",
        body: "DAG = Directed Acyclic Graph. Meaning: directed + no cycles.\n\nExample: A -> B -> D, A -> C -> E. There is no way to start at a node and eventually return to it." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Why does DAG DP exist?",
        body: "Consider: A -> B -> D, A -> C -> D. Suppose every edge has a cost. You want: minimum cost from A to D.\n\nThere are two paths: A->B->D and A->C->D. You could solve this using shortest-path techniques.\n\nBut because the graph has no cycles, there is something powerful: we can process nodes in dependency order. That's where Topological Sort + DP comes in." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. The mental model",
        body: "Think about a DAG as a chain of dependencies:\nA -> B -> D\nA -> C -> D\n\nD depends on B and C. So before computing the answer for D, we should process B and C. Topological order gives us exactly that: A, B, C, D.\n\nThen: DP[A] -> DP[B], DP[C] -> DP[D]. This is the key." },
      { type: "text", heading: "4. Why DAG makes DP easier",
        body: "In general graphs, a cycle (A->B->C->A) means A depends on B, B depends on C, C depends on A. There is no natural 'first'.\n\nBut in a DAG (A->B->C->D), dependencies always move forward. Therefore: topological order = dependency order. And that's exactly what DP needs." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Core DAG DP pattern", dark: true,
        body: "1. Topologically sort the graph.\n2. Initialize DP.\n3. Process nodes in topological order.\n4. Relax/update neighbors.\n\nFor shortest path: dp[next] = min(dp[next], dp[current] + weight)\nFor longest path:  dp[next] = max(dp[next], dp[current] + weight)\nFor counting:      dp[next] += dp[current]" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "6. The three most important DAG DP types",
        intro: "You should recognize these immediately:",
        items: ["Type 1: Shortest path in DAG", "Type 2: Longest path in DAG", "Type 3: Number of ways / paths in DAG"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. DAG shortest path",
        body: "Suppose: A->B=5, A->C=2, C->B=1, B->D=3.\n\nTopological order: A -> C -> B -> D.\n\nInitialize: dist[A]=0, everything else = INF.\n\nProcess A: dist[B]=5, dist[C]=2.\nProcess C: dist[B] = min(5, 2+1) = 3.\nProcess B: dist[D] = 3+3 = 6.\n\nAnswer: A -> C -> B -> D, cost = 6." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "8. Why this is better than Dijkstra here", tone: "gold",
        body: "Dijkstra requires a PriorityQueue, because it needs to repeatedly choose the smallest distance. A DAG already gives us topological order, so we can process nodes directly.",
        conclusion: "DAG shortest path = Topological Sort + Relaxation. Time: O(V+E). Excellent." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Important: DAG can have negative edges", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a beautiful property. Suppose: A->B=5, B->C=-10. Dijkstra can't safely handle arbitrary negative edges. Bellman-Ford can.\n\nBut if the graph is a DAG: negative edge weights are fine, because there are no cycles. We simply process nodes in topological order.",
        conclusion: "DAG + negative edges -> DAG shortest path still works. Important interview point." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. DAG longest path",
        body: "Finding the longest path in a general graph is difficult. But: longest path in a DAG can be solved efficiently with DP.\n\nSuppose: A->B->D, A->C->D, with A->B=5, A->C=2, B->D=4, C->D=10.\n\nThen: A->B->D = 9, A->C->D = 12. So: dp[D] = 12.\n\nTransition: dp[next] = max(dp[next], dp[current] + weight)." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Why longest path is hard in general graphs",
        body: "Suppose: A -> B -> C -> A (a cycle). You could keep walking around the cycle. If weights are positive, the path can grow indefinitely, so longest path becomes problematic.\n\nDAG removes this issue: no cycle -> finite number of paths -> DP works." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Pattern: Number of Paths", tone: "gold",
        body: "One of the most important DAG DP patterns. Suppose: A->B, A->C, B->D, C->D. How many ways from A to D? A->B->D and A->C->D. Answer: 2.\n\nDP: dp[A]=1. Then dp[B]+=dp[A], dp[C]+=dp[A]. Then dp[D]+=dp[B], dp[D]+=dp[C].",
        conclusion: "Therefore: dp[D] = 2." },
      { type: "text", heading: "13. The mental model for counting",
        body: "Whenever you see: number of ways, number of paths, how many possible routes, ways to reach destination -- in a DAG, think: dp[source] = 1, then dp[next] += dp[current]. Very important DP pattern." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. DAG DP + Topological Sort", dark: true,
        body: "DAG\n |\nTopological order\n |\ndp[source] = initial value\n |\nprocess current\n |\nupdate neighbors\n\nMinimum: dp[next] = min(dp[next], candidate)\nMaximum: dp[next] = max(dp[next], candidate)\nCounting: dp[next] += dp[current]" },
      { type: "text", heading: "15. This is the DP pattern you should learn",
        body: "State: dp[node]. Transition: dp[next] = operation(dp[next], dp[node]).\n\nThe graph tells us which states depend on which other states. Topological sort tells us what order to evaluate them. This is exactly the same idea as ordinary DP." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "16. Compare DAG DP with normal DP", items: [
        { label: "Normal 1D DP", body: "dp[i] depends on dp[i-1]. Process i = 0 -> n. Fixed dependency order." },
        { label: "DAG DP", body: "dp[node] depends on dp[parent nodes]. Topological ordering tells us parent -> child. Graph-derived dependency order." }
      ], conclusion: "This connection is extremely important." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Pattern A — Course Scheduling",
        body: "Suppose A -> B means A must be completed before B. This is a DAG dependency graph.\n\nIf the question asks 'can I complete everything?', use Topological Sort. If it asks 'what is the maximum/minimum/number of ways through the dependency graph?', then: Topological Sort + DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18. Pattern B — Critical Path", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose a project has Task A=3 days, Task B=5 days, Task C=2 days, with dependencies A->B, A->C, B->D, C->D. Question: minimum project completion time?\n\nIf tasks can run in parallel, the answer depends on the longest dependency chain.",
        conclusion: "DAG + task durations + longest dependency path -> DAG DP. This is essentially the critical path concept." },
      { type: "text", heading: "19. Pattern C — Build Systems",
        body: "Imagine: Library A -> Library B -> Application. Each edge is a dependency. You might need: longest dependency chain / build time. Again: DAG + DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "20. Pattern D — Maximum Score",
        body: "Suppose: A->B->D, A->C->D. Each edge gives points. Question: maximum score from A to D?\n\ndp[next] = max(dp[next], dp[current] + score). Direct DAG DP pattern." },
      { type: "text", heading: "21. Pattern E — Minimum Cost",
        body: "Same structure: dp[next] = min(dp[next], dp[current] + cost).\n\nDon't memorize separate algorithms. Understand: DAG -> topological order -> DP transition. The operation (min, max, +) changes according to the problem." },
      { type: "text", heading: "22. Pattern F — Path Reconstruction",
        body: "Suppose you need the actual longest/shortest path. Maintain: parent[next] = current, when updating dp[next]. Then reconstruct: target -> parent -> parent -> ... -> source. Same technique you've seen in BFS/Dijkstra." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. DAG DP + negative weights",
        body: "Example: A->B=5, A->C=2, C->B=-10. Then: A->C->B = 2 - 10 = -8. DAG shortest path handles this perfectly.\n\nWhy? Because: no cycles + topological order = no repeated improvement problem." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "24. DAG DP vs Bellman-Ford", items: [
        { label: "Bellman-Ford", body: "General graph + negative edges. Complexity: O(VE)." },
        { label: "DAG shortest path", body: "DAG + negative edges. Complexity: O(V+E)." }
      ], conclusion: "So if you know the graph is a DAG: exploit the DAG structure. This is exactly the kind of optimization interviewers like." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "25. DAG DP vs Dijkstra", items: [
        { label: "Dijkstra", body: "Non-negative weights + general graph. ~O(E log V)." },
        { label: "DAG DP", body: "DAG + even negative weights. O(V+E)." }
      ], conclusion: "A DAG gives you a powerful shortcut." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "26. The most important recognition rule", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see: directed graph + no cycles + optimization/counting, think: DAG DP.",
        conclusion: "Then: Topological Sort -> DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "27. DAG DP decision tree", dark: true,
        body: "             DIRECTED GRAPH\n                   |\n               Has cycles?\n                   |\n          +--------+--------+\n          v                 v\n         YES                NO\n          |                 |\n          v                 v\nGeneral graph              DAG\n                            |\n                            v\n                    Topological order\n                            |\n                    +-------+--------+\n                    v       v        v\n                  Min      Max     Count\n                    v       v        v\n                  DP      DP       DP" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28. Important trap — longest path in a general graph", tone: "red",
        body: "If you see 'find the longest path', do NOT immediately think DAG DP. First ask: is the graph guaranteed to be a DAG?\n\nIf yes: DAG DP. If no: the problem may be much harder, potentially involving cycles, visited-state constraints, exponential search, or special graph structure.",
        conclusion: "The DAG guarantee is critical." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "29. Important trap — Topological Sort alone isn't DP", tone: "red",
        body: "Suppose: can all courses be completed? You need Topological Sort. But: what is the minimum completion time? Now you may need Topological Sort + DP.",
        conclusion: "This distinction matters." },
      { type: "text", heading: "30. DAG DP + State",
        body: "Advanced problems can have dp[node][state]. For example dp[node][k], where k represents: number of resources used, number of special nodes visited, number of operations, or remaining budget. Then: DAG + state + DP. A powerful combination." },
      { type: "text", heading: "31. DAG DP + Bitmask",
        body: "Very advanced: dp[node][mask], where mask tracks visited/selected objects. If the underlying graph is a DAG, you can sometimes combine topological order + bitmask DP. Don't prioritize this now." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "32. Complexity",
        body: "Topological sorting: O(V + E). DP traversal: O(V + E). Overall: O(V + E). Space: O(V + E) including graph and DP." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "33. Must-master problems", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Must Master", note: "Shortest Path in DAG, Longest Path in DAG, Number of Paths in DAG, Course Schedule / dependency ordering, Critical Path / project scheduling." },
        { id: "2", title: "Should Know", note: "DAG + negative weights, DAG path reconstruction, DAG + state." },
        { id: "3", title: "Advanced", note: "DAG + bitmask, DAG + complex state DP." }
      ]},
      { type: "practice", heading: "34-36. Interview answer templates",
        intro: "Shortest Path:",
        items: [
          "\"Because the graph is a DAG, I don't need Dijkstra. I'll first obtain a topological ordering, initialize the source distance to zero, and then process each node in topological order. For every outgoing edge, I'll relax the destination using dist[next] = min(dist[next], dist[current] + weight). This runs in O(V+E) and also works with negative edge weights because the graph has no cycles.\"",
          "Longest Path: \"Since the graph is a DAG, the longest path can be solved with dynamic programming. I'll topologically order the nodes and maintain dp[node] as the maximum score or distance reaching that node. For each edge, I'll update dp[next] = max(dp[next], dp[current] + weight). The DAG guarantees there are no cycles, so the DP is well-defined.\"",
          "Number of Paths: \"I'll use topological order to process each node after its predecessors. I initialize dp[source] = 1, because there's one way to be at the source. For every edge u -> v, I add dp[u] to dp[v]. At the end, dp[target] gives the number of paths.\""
        ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "37. Muscle-memory card", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you hear: 'DAG', 'directed acyclic', 'dependency graph', 'longest path in DAG', 'shortest path in DAG', 'number of paths', 'critical path', 'maximum score' -- think: Topological Sort + DP.\n\nThen: topological order -> process current -> update neighbor.",
        conclusion: "Choose transition: minimum -> min(). maximum -> max(). count -> +." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "38. The entire Tier 4 graph family", dark: true,
        body: "GRAPH\n |\n +-- Traversal: DFS, BFS, Grid DFS/BFS, Multi-Source BFS\n |\n +-- Connectivity: Union Find\n |\n +-- Ordering: Topological Sort\n |\n +-- Shortest Path: BFS, 0-1 BFS, Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, Floyd-Warshall, DAG Shortest Path\n |\n +-- Minimum Connection: MST (Kruskal, Prim)\n |\n +-- Graph Properties: Bipartite, SCC\n |\n +-- Graph + DP: DAG DP" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "Tier 4 muscle-memory map", items: [
        { label: "Explore graph", body: "DFS/BFS" },
        { label: "Shortest unweighted path", body: "BFS" },
        { label: "Multiple starting points", body: "Multi-source BFS" },
        { label: "0/1 cost", body: "0-1 BFS" },
        { label: "Non-negative weighted shortest path", body: "Dijkstra" },
        { label: "Negative edges", body: "Bellman-Ford" },
        { label: "All-pairs shortest path", body: "Floyd-Warshall" },
        { label: "Connect ALL, minimum total cost", body: "MST (Kruskal/Prim)" },
        { label: "Two groups / conflicts", body: "Bipartite" },
        { label: "Mutual reachability, directed graph", body: "SCC" },
        { label: "Dependency ordering", body: "Topological Sort" },
        { label: "DAG + min/max/count", body: "DAG DP" }
      ]},
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 4 — COMPLETE!",
        items: ["23. Graph DFS", "24. Graph BFS", "25. Grid DFS/BFS", "26. Multi-Source BFS", "27. Topological Sort", "28. Union Find", "29. Dijkstra", "30. 0-1 BFS", "31. Bellman-Ford", "32. Floyd-Warshall", "33. MST", "34. Bipartite Graph", "35. SCC", "36. DAG DP"] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Pattern Recognition Practice",
        points: ["Tier 4's entire graph family is complete -- 14 patterns covering traversal, connectivity, ordering, shortest path, minimum connection, graph properties, and graph+DP", "The bigger goal is not memorizing 36 algorithms: read the problem -> identify the mathematical structure -> identify the pattern -> choose the algorithm -> implement", "Next phase: mixed problems without being told the pattern -- identify structure, pattern, reasoning, algorithm, and complexity yourself", "That's where the real interview-ready 'beast mode' preparation starts"] }
    ]
  },

  "trie": {
    title: "Pattern 37 — Trie: The Prefix Tree (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Trie is one of the most important structures for: strings, prefixes, dictionaries, autocomplete, word search, prefix matching.",
        conclusion: "The biggest recognition trigger: the problem repeatedly asks about prefixes of strings. Think: Trie." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Trie exist?",
        body: "Suppose you have: apple, app, application, apply, banana, bat. And the interviewer asks: does any word start with \"app\"?\n\nWith a HashSet, set.contains(\"app\") only answers whether the complete word exists. It doesn't naturally represent app, apple, application, apply as a shared structure.\n\nTrie does." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model",
        body: "A Trie is essentially a Character Tree. For apple, app, apply you get:\n\n        a\n        |\n        p\n        |\n        p\n       / \\\n      l   END\n      |\n      e\n      |\n      END\n\nThe prefix \"app\" is physically shared. That's the fundamental reason Trie exists." },
      { type: "text", heading: "3. Trie = Prefix Tree",
        body: "Think: Trie -> shared prefixes -> fast prefix operations.\n\nInstead of storing apple, application, apply as completely independent strings, the Trie stores: a -> p -> p -> (l..., END)." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see:",
        items: ["prefix", "starts with", "autocomplete", "dictionary", "word prefixes", "all words beginning with X", "search words by prefix", "longest prefix", "shortest unique prefix", "word dictionary"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Trie node",
        body: "class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    boolean isWord;\n}\n\nEach node represents a character/prefix. isWord tells us: does a complete word end here? For app + apple, at the second p, isWord = true because \"app\" is a valid word, but the path continues app -> l -> e for \"apple\"." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "6. Basic operations",
        intro: "A Trie typically supports:",
        items: ["insert(word)", "search(word)", "startsWith(prefix)"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. Insert",
        body: "Insert \"apple\" character by character: a -> p -> p -> l -> e.\n\nAt the end: isWord = true." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "8. Search", tone: "gold",
        body: "Search \"app\": walk a -> p -> p, then check isWord == true.",
        conclusion: "Important: if \"apple\" exists but \"app\" doesn't, isWord at app = false, so search(\"app\") = false even though the prefix exists." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "9. startsWith vs search", items: [
        { label: "search()", body: "path exists + isWord must be true" },
        { label: "startsWith()", body: "path exists is enough -- we don't care about isWord" }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is extremely important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Complexity",
        body: "For a word of length L: insert = O(L), search = O(L), prefix = O(L).\n\nThis is one of the biggest advantages -- the operation depends on word length rather than number of words." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "11. Trie vs HashSet", items: [
        { label: "HashSet", body: "Great for: does exact word exist? O(L) average lookup." },
        { label: "Trie", body: "Great for: does prefix exist? What words start with X? Autocomplete. Prefix relationships." }
      ], conclusion: "Exact lookup -> HashSet. Prefix lookup -> Trie. Don't automatically use Trie for every string problem." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "12. Trie vs HashMap (child storage)", items: [
        { label: "Array: children[26]", body: "Advantages: fast, predictable, good for lowercase English letters." },
        { label: "HashMap<Character, TrieNode>", body: "Advantages: supports arbitrary characters, saves space when branching is sparse." }
      ], conclusion: "Interview question: \"Why did you choose an array?\" Answer: \"The input alphabet is fixed to lowercase English letters, so an array gives constant-time child access with simpler implementation.\"" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Pattern A — Autocomplete",
        body: "Suppose user types \"app\". Dictionary contains: apple, application, apply, appstore.\n\nTrie:\napp\n |\n +-- le\n +-- lication\n +-- ly\n +-- store\n\nYou: 1) walk to \"app\". 2) DFS from that Trie node. 3) collect words. This becomes: Trie + DFS." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Pattern B — Prefix Search", dark: true,
        body: "Question: find all words beginning with \"pre\".\n\nUse: Trie -> walk to \"pre\" -> DFS -> collect words.\n\nVery common combination: Trie + DFS." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15. Pattern C — Longest Prefix",
        body: "Suppose dictionary: a, app, apple, banana. Input: \"application\".\n\nWalk through Trie: a, p, p, l, e. The longest valid dictionary prefix is \"apple\".\n\nSo: Trie + greedy traversal." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "16. Pattern D — Word Dictionary With Wildcards", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose search(\"b..\") means: any two characters after b. Trie can handle this.\n\nAt a normal character 'c', follow only children['c']. At wildcard '.', we must explore ALL children.",
        conclusion: "So: Trie + DFS/backtracking. This is a very important combination." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17. Pattern E — Word Search II", tone: "gold",
        body: "One of the most important Trie problems. You have a grid of letters and words to find (oath, pea, eat, rain). Naively: for every word, run DFS on grid -- this repeats huge amounts of work.\n\nInstead: words -> Trie, then grid DFS follows the Trie simultaneously.",
        conclusion: "This gives: Trie + Backtracking + Grid DFS. Very important." },
      { type: "text", heading: "18. Why Trie + Backtracking works",
        body: "Suppose you're exploring o -> a -> t. If the Trie says \"oat\" doesn't exist as a prefix: STOP immediately.\n\nWithout Trie, you might continue exploring useless paths. So Trie acts as search-space pruning. This is a major idea used repeatedly in backtracking." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19. Trie + Backtracking mental model", dark: true,
        body: "Grid\n |\nchoose next cell\n |\nTrie tells whether prefix is valid\n |\nif invalid -> prune\n |\ncontinue\n\nSo: Backtracking + Trie pruning can transform a huge search into a manageable one." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. Pattern F — Maximum XOR", tone: "gold",
        body: "An unusual but important Trie pattern. Suppose nums = [3, 10, 5, 25, 2, 8]. Need: maximum XOR of two numbers.\n\nA bitwise Trie can store binary representations (25 = 11001, 10 = 01010, ...). For each bit, to maximize XOR, prefer the opposite bit -- if current bit is 1, prefer 0, and vice versa.",
        conclusion: "This gives: Bitwise Trie." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. Trie variations", dark: true,
        body: "Trie\n |\n +-- Basic Trie\n +-- Prefix Search\n +-- Autocomplete\n +-- Trie + DFS\n +-- Trie + Backtracking\n +-- Trie + Wildcard\n +-- Trie + Frequency\n +-- Bitwise Trie" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22. When NOT to use Trie", tone: "red",
        body: "Don't use Trie just because the problem contains strings.\n\nExact string lookup -> HashSet/HashMap. Character frequency -> HashMap/int[26]. Anagram -> frequency. Longest substring -> usually Sliding Window. String DP -> usually DP.",
        conclusion: "Trie is specifically powerful when: prefix structure matters." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Trie + other patterns", dark: true,
        body: "Trie\n +-- + DFS\n +-- + Backtracking\n +-- + BFS\n +-- + HashMap\n +-- + Heap\n +-- + Frequency\n +-- + Bit manipulation\n\nThe most important: Trie + Backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "24. Trie muscle-memory card", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see: prefix, startsWith, autocomplete, dictionary, words beginning with, prefix matching, word search with many words -- think: Trie.",
        conclusion: "Then ask: Exact word? -> HashSet may be enough. Prefix? -> Trie. Many words + grid? -> Trie + Backtracking. Wildcard? -> Trie + DFS. Maximum XOR? -> Bitwise Trie." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true },
          { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: false }, { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: false },
          { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: false },
          { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: false }, { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false },
          { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "45. Subsets", done: false },
          { label: "46. Permutations", done: false }, { label: "47. Combinations", done: false },
          { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: false }, { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false },
          { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false }, { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Trie + Prefix Search",
        points: ["We'll go deeper into the prefix-query mindset: prefix -> Trie node -> DFS/BFS -> all matching words", "Including autocomplete, top-K suggestions, frequency-ranked prefixes, shortest unique prefix, and Trie + Heap"] }
    ]
  },

  "trie-prefix-search": {
    title: "Pattern 38 — Trie + Prefix Search: Autocomplete, Top-K & Word Break (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is where Trie becomes genuinely useful in interviews. The core pattern: find the Trie node representing a prefix, then explore everything below it.",
        conclusion: "Prefix -> Trie -> Prefix Node -> DFS/BFS -> Matching Words." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. Why does this pattern exist?",
        body: "Suppose the dictionary contains: apple, application, apply, app, banana, bat, ball. Query: \"app\". You want: app, apple, application, apply.\n\nA HashSet doesn't naturally answer: \"give me every word beginning with app.\" Trie does." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model",
        body: "Imagine the Trie:\n                 root\n                  |\n                  a\n                  |\n                  p\n                  |\n                  p\n              /   |   \\\n             l    END  ...\n             |\n             e\n\nWhen searching \"app\", you walk root -> a -> p -> p. Now you're standing at the Prefix Node.\n\nEverything below this node represents words starting with \"app\". That's the key insight." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. The algorithm", dark: true,
        body: "Step 1: Walk the prefix \"app\".\nStep 2: If any character doesn't exist, the prefix doesn't exist -- return empty.\nStep 3: Once you reach the prefix node, run DFS(node).\nStep 4: Whenever isWord == true, you found a matching word." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Example",
        body: "Dictionary: apple, app, application, apply, banana. Query: \"app\".\n\nTrie traversal: root -> a -> p -> p. At this point isWord = true, so \"app\" is a result.\n\nThen DFS:\napp -> l -> e (apple)\napp -> l -> i (application)\napp -> l -> y (apply)\n\nResult: app, apple, application, apply." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "5. The most important separation", items: [
        { label: "Prefix navigation", body: "findNode(prefix) -- walk the Trie to the node representing the prefix." },
        { label: "Result enumeration", body: "collectWords(node) -- DFS from that node to gather every complete word below it." }
      ], conclusion: "This separation makes Trie problems much easier to reason about." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6-7. findNode(prefix) + DFS to collect words", dark: true,
        body: "TrieNode node = root;\nfor (char c : prefix.toCharArray()) {\n    if (node.children[c - 'a'] == null) return null;\n    node = node.children[c - 'a'];\n}\nreturn node;\n\nThen from the prefix node:\nvoid collect(TrieNode node, StringBuilder current, List<String> result) {\n    if (node.isWord) result.add(current.toString());\n    // explore children[0..25]\n}" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "8. Recognition trigger", tone: "gold",
        body: "When the problem says: \"given a dictionary, return all words that start with a given prefix,\" immediate thought: Trie + DFS.",
        conclusion: "Not: for every word, startsWith(prefix) -- unless constraints are tiny." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Why Trie is efficient",
        body: "Suppose N = 1,000,000 words and prefix = \"inter\". A naive approach checks every word: roughly O(N x L).\n\nTrie: walk prefix + explore matching subtree: roughly O(P + K x L), where P = prefix length, K = number of returned words, L = output word length.\n\nThe important point: you don't inspect unrelated prefixes." },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Pattern A — Autocomplete",
        body: "The classic real-world use. User types \"ama\". Trie contains: amazon, amazing, amalgam. Walk a -> m -> a, then explore subtree. That's autocomplete." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11. Autocomplete with ranking", tone: "gold",
        body: "Real autocomplete usually doesn't return arbitrary words. Suppose apple->5000, application->3000, apply->1000. Query \"app\" should return them ranked by frequency, not arbitrary order.",
        conclusion: "Now the pattern becomes: Trie + Heap." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Trie + Top-K", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose the question says: return the top 5 most frequent words with prefix \"app\". The Trie gives you all matching words. The Heap gives you top K.",
        conclusion: "So: Trie + DFS + MinHeap/MaxHeap. A very important combination." },
      { type: "text", heading: "13. Why a heap?",
        body: "Suppose there are 100,000 matching words but you only need K = 10. You don't need to sort all 100,000. Maintain a min-heap of size K: when a better candidate arrives, remove smallest, add candidate.\n\nComplexity becomes approximately O(M log K), where M is the number of matching words examined." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Trie + frequency stored at node",
        body: "Sometimes each Trie node can store an int frequency, or a Map<String, Integer> depending on design. For a complete word: isWord = true, frequency = X. Then DFS can access ranking information." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15. Advanced optimization — store Top-K at every Trie node", tone: "gold",
        body: "Excellent system-design idea. Instead of performing DFS every time (prefix -> DFS huge subtree -> heap), you can maintain node.topK while inserting/updating words. Then query(\"app\") becomes: walk to app -> return node.topK. Very fast.",
        conclusion: "This is the kind of optimization you might discuss in a Google/Uber senior-level interview if asked to design autocomplete at scale." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "16. Tradeoff", items: [
        { label: "Store top K at each node", body: "Query: very fast. Insertion/update: more expensive. Memory: higher." },
        { label: "DFS + heap on every query", body: "Query: slower for large subtrees. Insertion: cheap. Memory: lower." }
      ], conclusion: "Read-heavy workload -> precompute top K. Broader engineering principle: move work from query time to write time when reads dominate." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17-19. Pattern B — Shortest Unique Prefix",
        body: "Classic Trie problem. Suppose: apple, ape, april, banana, bat.\n\nFor \"apple\", what is the shortest prefix uniquely identifying it? Maybe \"app\". For \"banana\", maybe \"ban\".\n\nStore prefixCount at each Trie node. At prefix \"ap\", count might be 3 (apple/ape/april all pass through). At \"app\", count = 1.\n\nTherefore \"app\" is the shortest unique prefix for \"apple\".\n\nMental model: for each word, walk characters, find first node where prefixCount == 1 -- that prefix is unique. This is: Trie + Frequency." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20. Pattern C — Prefix Frequency",
        body: "Suppose: apple, app, application, banana. Query \"app\": how many words start with \"app\"? Store prefixCount at each Trie node. Then: walk to \"app\" -> return prefixCount. No DFS required. Important optimization." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "21. Exact word count vs prefix count", items: [
        { label: "wordCount", body: "How many times was \"app\" inserted exactly? (exact question)" },
        { label: "prefixCount", body: "How many words start with \"app\"? (prefix question)" }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is frequently useful." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22-23. Pattern D — Longest Common Prefix (and an important judgment call)", tone: "red",
        body: "Given flower, flow, flight, find \"fl\". You don't necessarily need a Trie -- direct character comparison works fine. A Trie can model it (common prefix ends where branching occurs or isWord == true), but for LCP, simple string comparison is usually better than constructing a Trie.",
        conclusion: "Don't think 'I learned Trie, therefore I should use Trie.' Instead: use Trie when its prefix-sharing structure provides a real advantage." },
      { type: "text", heading: "24. Pattern E — Search All Prefixes",
        body: "Suppose word = \"apple\" and you need to find which prefixes are dictionary words. Walk a, ap, app, appl, apple. At every node, if isWord: prefix is a valid word.\n\nExample dictionary: a, app, apple -- returns: a, app, apple." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Pattern F — Prefix Replacement",
        body: "Suppose dictionary: cat, bat, rat. Sentence: \"the cattle was running\". Replace each word with its shortest dictionary root: \"the cat was running\".\n\nFor each word: walk Trie -> first isWord -> stop. This is: Trie + Greedy Prefix Search." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "26-27. Pattern G — Word Break + Trie", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose dictionary: leet, code. Input: \"leetcode\". Question: can it be segmented? Trie can efficiently identify dictionary words starting at each position.\n\nThen: Trie + DP. Example: dp[i] = can string[0..i) be segmented? At each position, walk Trie forward and update DP when a complete word is found.",
        conclusion: "Mental model: String -> Trie finds valid next words -> DP remembers reachable positions. This becomes especially useful when the dictionary is large." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28-29. Pattern H — Trie + Backtracking", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose: grid + many dictionary words. Naive: for each word, DFS grid. Better: words -> Trie, then grid DFS follows a Trie prefix check -- invalid prefix? PRUNE.\n\nImagine backtracking explores a -> x -> q. If no dictionary word starts with \"axq\", STOP. You don't need to explore axq... further.",
        conclusion: "This means Trie answers: \"is this partial solution still potentially valid?\" That is exactly what backtracking needs. One of the most powerful combinations in this tier." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "30. Trie + Backtracking = search space reduction", dark: true,
        body: "Without Trie:\n             huge search tree\n            /  /  /  /  /  /\n           /  /  /  /  /  /\n\nWith Trie:\n             search\n              |\n        prefix valid?\n          /       \\\n        YES        NO\n         |          |\n       continue    PRUNE" },
      { type: "text", heading: "31. Trie + BFS",
        body: "Less common, but possible. Suppose you need to search strings by length/state -- you might combine Trie + BFS, for shortest transformation-like problems, dictionary state search, or autocomplete by levels.\n\nBut: Trie + DFS/backtracking is much more important." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "32. When NOT to use Trie", tone: "red",
        body: "Exact lookup -> HashSet. Frequency -> HashMap/int[26]. Prefix only, one/few strings -> sometimes String.startsWith() is enough. Longest substring -> Sliding Window. String edit distance -> DP. Palindrome -> usually Two pointers/DP/Manacher.",
        conclusion: "Don't force Trie." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "33. Complete prefix-Trie pattern map", dark: true,
        body: "                    TRIE\n                      |\n             Prefix-based problem\n                      |\n          +-----------+------------+\n          v           v            v\n       Exact?       Prefix?      Many words\n          v           v            v\n      HashSet       Trie        Trie\n                      |            |\n          +-----------+------+     v\n          v           v      v   Backtracking\n      Count         All      Top-K\n      prefixes      words\n          v           v        v\n       Counter       DFS      Heap\n\nAnd:\nTrie\n +-- + DFS\n +-- + Backtracking\n +-- + Heap\n +-- + DP\n +-- + Bitwise" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "34. Complexity you should be able to explain",
        body: "P = prefix length, K = number of matching words, L = average result length.\n\nFind prefix node: O(P). Enumerate results: roughly O(K x L). So: O(P + K x L).\n\nKey interview insight: the Trie makes prefix navigation proportional to the prefix length rather than scanning the entire dictionary." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "35-37. Interview answer templates",
        intro: "Autocomplete:",
        items: [
          "\"Because the queries are prefix-based, I'd use a Trie so all words sharing a prefix share the same path. For each query, I walk the Trie to the prefix node and then traverse its subtree to collect matching words. If I need only the top K suggestions, I can rank the words with a heap, or for a read-heavy system, precompute top-K suggestions at each Trie node.\"",
          "Unique Prefix: \"I'll store a count of how many words pass through each Trie node. For a given word, I'll walk its characters and return the first prefix whose count becomes one. That prefix uniquely identifies the word.\"",
          "Word Break: \"I'll use the Trie to efficiently find dictionary words beginning at each position in the string, and DP to record which string positions are reachable. Whenever I reach a complete Trie word, I update the corresponding DP state.\""
        ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "38. Muscle-memory card", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see: prefix, startsWith, autocomplete, top K prefixes, prefix frequency, shortest unique prefix, all words with prefix -- think: Trie.",
        conclusion: "Then ask: need all matches? -> Trie + DFS. Need top K? -> Trie + Heap. Need prefix count? -> Trie + prefixCount. Need unique prefix? -> Trie + frequency. Need segmentation? -> Trie + DP. Need grid + many words? -> Trie + Backtracking. Need maximum XOR? -> Bitwise Trie." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: false }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: false }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: false },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: false }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: false },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: false }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: false },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Trie + DFS",
        points: ["Focus on the important difference between plain Trie traversal and Trie + DFS", "Building toward Word Search II, where the real combination becomes: Grid DFS + Backtracking + Trie + Pruning", "That combination is one of the highest-value patterns in this entire Tier 5"] }
    ]
  },

  "trie-dfs": {
    title: "Pattern 39 — Trie + DFS: Word Search II & the Backtracking Bridge (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Now we move from Trie = prefix data structure to Trie + DFS = explore all valid continuations of a prefix.",
        conclusion: "This is where Trie starts becoming a search-space engine." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Trie + DFS exist?",
        body: "Suppose you have: apple, app, application, apply, ape, bat, ball, banana. Query \"app\". The Trie gets you here: root -> a -> p -> p.\n\nBut now you need: everything below this node. That's DFS.\n\nSo: Trie -> find prefix node -> DFS subtree -> collect valid words." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "2. Mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Think of the Trie as a tree of possible future characters. At \"app\", the Trie tells you: app->END, app->l->e, app->l->y, app->l->i->...\n\nDFS asks: \"what are all the possible continuations from here?\"",
        conclusion: "Trie = constraints. DFS = exploration. This distinction is very important." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "3. Trie traversal vs Trie + DFS", items: [
        { label: "Simple Trie traversal", body: "You're looking for one path: app -> a -> p -> p. That's O(prefix length)." },
        { label: "Trie + DFS", body: "Now you want every word below \"app\" -- you need to explore branches. That's DFS." }
      ]},
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see:",
        items: ["Find all words matching prefix", "Generate all completions", "Autocomplete", "Dictionary subtree", "All possible words", "Explore valid prefixes", "Enumerate strings"],
        note: "But there's an even stronger trigger: \"Explore all valid continuations of a prefix.\"" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Basic structure", dark: true,
        body: "class TrieNode {\n    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];\n    boolean isWord;\n}\n\nvoid dfs(TrieNode node, StringBuilder path) {\n    if (node.isWord) result.add(path.toString());\n    for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {\n        if (node.children[i] != null) {\n            path.append((char)('a' + i));\n            dfs(node.children[i], path);\n            path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nNotice the important part: choose -> recurse -> undo. That is already: Backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "6. Important connection", tone: "gold",
        body: "You may notice something: \"this looks like backtracking.\" Exactly. Trie + DFS naturally introduces the backtracking pattern: Trie -> DFS -> choose character -> explore -> undo character.",
        conclusion: "So Trie and Backtracking are deeply connected." },
      { type: "text", heading: "7. The path variable",
        body: "Suppose you're at a->p->p->l->e. Your StringBuilder contains \"apple\". When returning from 'e', you undo 'e'. Then explore another branch 'y', giving \"apply\". That's the classic Choose -> Explore -> Undo pattern." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "8. Why StringBuilder?", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't repeatedly create path + character, because that creates many temporary strings. Instead use a StringBuilder with append() and deleteCharAt().",
        conclusion: "This makes the backtracking structure clearer and more efficient." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Example",
        body: "Trie contains: app, apple, apply, ape. Starting at prefix \"ap\":\n\nap\n +-- p\n |    +-- END\n |    +-- l\n |         +-- e\n |         +-- y\n +-- e\n      +-- END\n\nDFS outputs: app, apple, apply, ape." },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Pattern A — Autocomplete",
        body: "The most direct application. Query \"ap\". Process: findNode(\"ap\") -> DFS -> collect words. So: Autocomplete = Trie + DFS." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Pattern B — All Words With Prefix",
        body: "Question: return every dictionary word beginning with \"trans\". Algorithm: find \"trans\" -> DFS subtree -> collect isWord nodes. Straightforward Trie + DFS." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12-13. Pattern C — Lexicographical Order", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose children are explored 'a' -> 'b' -> 'c' -> ... -> 'z', then DFS naturally produces lexicographical order. Example: app, apple, application, apply, banana, bat.\n\nA Trie is already organized by characters. If you traverse children[0], children[1], ... children[25], you're effectively doing 'a', 'b', ..., 'z'.",
        conclusion: "So you don't necessarily need sort(result) if the Trie traversal is performed alphabetically. An important optimization." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "14. Pattern D — Count Words With Prefix", tone: "gold",
        body: "You don't necessarily need DFS. If you store prefixCount at each node: findNode(prefix) -> return prefixCount is much faster.",
        conclusion: "Use DFS when you need the actual words; use stored counts when you only need the count." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15. Pattern E — Top-K Autocomplete",
        body: "Suppose app, apple, application, apply each have a frequency. Question: return top 3 suggestions. Now: Trie -> DFS -> Heap, or precompute topK at each Trie node. This is: Trie + DFS + Heap." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "16-17. Pattern F — Wildcard Search", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose search(\"b..\"). At 'b', follow only children['b']. At '.', you don't know the character, so explore ALL children. That's DFS.\n\nSuppose at first wildcard: b -> a, e, i, o. You must try ba, be, bi, bo... This is a search tree.",
        conclusion: "So: Wildcard -> branching -> DFS -> backtracking. Trie + DFS + Wildcard branching." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "18-19. Pattern G — Word Search II",
        body: "The important one. Given a grid and a dictionary of words, find all dictionary words that appear in the grid.\n\nGrid: o a a n / e t a e / i h k r / i f l v. Dictionary: oath, pea, eat, rain. Answer: oath, eat.\n\nNaive: for each word, run DFS on grid. If W = number of words, you're repeatedly searching the grid -- lots of repeated work." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. Better solution", tone: "gold",
        body: "Build Trie(dictionary). Then: for every grid cell, DFS(cell, trieRoot). During DFS: current grid character -> Trie child? NO -> STOP. YES -> continue.",
        conclusion: "This is the key optimization." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. The search tree",
        body: "Suppose you start at 'o'. Trie tells you: o -> a -> t -> h, and other branches.\n\nGrid DFS explores neighboring cells. If the current path is o->a->t and Trie says \"oat\" is a valid prefix: continue.\n\nIf you reach o->a->x and no Trie path exists: PRUNE immediately." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "22. This is the big pattern", tone: "gold",
        body: "You should memorize: Many words + Grid + Find words = Trie + Backtracking.",
        conclusion: "Not: DFS independently for every word." },
      { type: "text", heading: "23. Why Trie is so powerful here",
        body: "Imagine 10,000 words. Many begin with \"pre...\". Instead of checking each word independently, Trie shares p -> r -> e. All those searches share the same prefix. That's the fundamental optimization." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "24. Backtracking structure", dark: true,
        body: "void dfs(int r, int c, TrieNode node) {\n\nAt each step:\nChoose: character at grid[r][c]\nCheck: Trie child exists?\nMark: visited\nExplore: 4 neighbors\nUndo: unmark visited\n\nThis is classic backtracking." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Why mark visited?",
        body: "Suppose grid A B / C D. You are at A and move A -> B -> D -> C. You shouldn't immediately return C -> A, because the same cell can't be reused in the same word path.\n\nSo: visited[r][c] = true before recursion, then visited[r][c] = false after recursion." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "26. In-place visited optimization", dark: true,
        body: "Instead of boolean[][] visited, you can temporarily modify the grid:\n\nchar temp = board[r][c];\nboard[r][c] = '#';\ndfs(...);\nboard[r][c] = temp;\n\nThis saves extra space. But in an interview: use whichever implementation is clearer unless memory constraints matter." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Pruning", tone: "gold",
        body: "Backtracking becomes powerful because of Pruning. Suppose prefix = \"xyz\" and Trie doesn't contain \"xyz\". Then: STOP. Don't explore xyza..., xyzb..., xyzc...",
        conclusion: "This can eliminate enormous portions of the search tree." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "28. General backtracking formula", dark: true,
        body: "This is a concept you should carry into every future problem:\n\nChoose\n |\nCheck constraint\n |\nExplore\n |\nUndo\n\nWith Trie:\nChoose character -> Trie validates prefix -> Explore -> Undo\n\nSo Trie becomes a constraint validator." },
      { type: "text", heading: "29. Pattern H — Trie + DFS + Memoization",
        body: "Sometimes the same Trie state and input position can be reached multiple times. Then you may have Trie node + position as a DP state. For example: memo[position][trieNode]. This is advanced, but conceptually: Trie + DFS + DP can eliminate repeated searches. Don't prioritize implementation yet." },
      { type: "text", heading: "30. Pattern I — Trie + DFS + Frequency",
        body: "Suppose you're asked: find the most frequent word matching a prefix. You can: Trie -> DFS -> compare frequencies. No heap required if you only need one best result. For top K: Trie + DFS + Heap." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "31. When NOT to use Trie + DFS", tone: "red",
        body: "One prefix, small dictionary: for word : words, if startsWith(prefix) may be enough. Only count: use prefixCount instead of traversing the whole subtree. Exact word search: use HashSet unless other Trie operations are also required. Longest substring: usually Sliding Window.",
        conclusion: "Word segmentation: often Trie + DP rather than plain DFS, because repeated states can occur." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "32. The core combinations", dark: true,
        body: "                  TRIE\n                   |\n           +-------+--------+\n           v       v        v\n          DFS   Backtrack   DP\n           |       |        |\n           v       v        v\n       Prefix    Grid      Word\n       Search   Search     Break\n\nAnd the most important compound pattern:\nTrie -> Backtracking -> Pruning" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "33. Complexity thinking", tone: "gold",
        body: "For basic Trie DFS: if there are N nodes in the relevant Trie subtree, O(N) plus output construction.\n\nFor Word Search II, worst-case complexity can still be large because you're exploring grid paths, but Trie pruning dramatically reduces practical search. Don't give an artificially simple O(V+E) answer here.",
        conclusion: "Instead explain: \"the worst case is exponential in grid path length, but the Trie prunes any path that isn't a prefix of a dictionary word, which substantially reduces the search space.\" That's a much better interview explanation." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "34. Interview answer — Word Search II",
        intro: "If asked: \"how would you optimize searching many words in a grid?\"",
        items: [
          "\"Instead of performing a separate DFS for every word, I'll build a Trie containing all dictionary words. Then I'll start DFS/backtracking from every grid cell while simultaneously traversing the Trie. If the current character doesn't exist as a Trie child, I immediately prune that path. This shares common prefixes across words and avoids exploring paths that cannot form any dictionary word.\""
        ],
        note: "That's exactly the kind of answer you want in a strong interview." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "35. Muscle-memory card", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see: Trie + \"all possibilities\" + \"explore children\" -- think: DFS.\n\nWhen you see: Trie + grid + many words -- think: Trie + Backtracking.\n\nWhen you see: current prefix not in Trie -- think: PRUNE.",
        conclusion: "And remember: Choose -> Trie validation -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: false }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: false },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: false }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: false },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: false }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: false },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Trie + Backtracking",
        points: ["This is where we'll make the connection explicit: Trie + Backtracking + Pruning + State restoration", "We'll use Word Search II as the main example and then generalize the pattern", "So you can recognize it in problems that don't mention Trie at all"] }
    ]
  },

  "trie-backtracking": {
    title: "Pattern 40 — Trie + Backtracking: Word Search II, Word Squares & Dynamic Pruning (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "One of the most valuable compound patterns in this entire section. The important thing is NOT to memorize Word Search II -- you want to recognize the general structure: I am exploring many possible choices, but I have a prefix/state structure that can tell me very early whether continuing is worthwhile.",
        conclusion: "That gives: Backtracking + Pruning. When the possibilities are strings: Trie + Backtracking." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does this pattern exist?",
        body: "Grid: o a a n / e t a e / i h k r / i f l v. Words: oath, pea, eat, rain. You need to find all words.\n\nA naive approach: for every word, search the grid. But the words share prefixes -- apple, application, apply all share \"app\". A Trie stores that shared structure once. Then backtracking explores the grid.\n\nSo: Dictionary -> Trie -> valid prefixes -> Grid Backtracking." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. The mental model", dark: true,
        body: "Think of the algorithm as two systems working together.\n\nTrie asks: \"is this prefix potentially useful?\"\nBacktracking asks: \"what happens if I choose this next character/cell?\"\n\nTogether:\nChoose next\n |\nTrie validation\n |\nInvalid?\n +--+--+\n YES   NO\n |      |\nPRUNE  Explore\n         |\n       Undo" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. The universal backtracking pattern", dark: true,
        body: "function search(state):\n    if solution:\n        record answer\n    for each choice:\n        make choice\n        if valid:\n            search(new state)\n        undo choice\n\nThe four words you should remember: Choose -> Validate -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "4. Trie changes \"Validate\"", items: [
        { label: "Normal backtracking", body: "if (constraint violated) return;" },
        { label: "Trie-based backtracking", body: "if (trie.children[c] == null) return;" }
      ], conclusion: "So: Backtracking + Trie constraint becomes extremely aggressive pruning." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Example",
        body: "Dictionary: cat, car, cart, dog. Trie:\nc\n +-- a\n |    +-- t\n |    +-- r\n |         +-- t\n +-- ...\n\nSuppose current path is c -> a. Trie says \"ca\" is a valid prefix. Continue.\n\nSuppose next choice creates c -> a -> x. Trie has no ca -> x. Therefore: stop immediately. No need to explore cax..., caxy..., caxyz..." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Why this is better than ordinary backtracking", dark: true,
        body: "Without Trie:\n                 all possible paths\n              / / / / / / / / / /\n             / / / / / / / / / /\n\nWith Trie:\n                  search\n                    |\n             prefix exists?\n              /           \\\n            YES            NO\n             |              |\n          continue        PRUNE\n\nThis is the central optimization." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. Word Search II — full pattern derivation", dark: true,
        body: "Step 1: we have many words -- words[].\nStep 2: words share prefixes -- therefore: Trie.\nStep 3: need to explore possible paths through a grid -- therefore: DFS/Backtracking.\nStep 4: need to avoid revisiting cells -- therefore: visited.\nStep 5: need to stop impossible prefixes immediately -- therefore: Trie pruning.\n\nFinal: Trie + DFS + Backtracking + Pruning." },
      { type: "text", heading: "8. State we maintain",
        body: "At any point we need: row, column, Trie node, current path, visited state. Conceptually: state = (r, c, trieNode, path).\n\nThe Trie node itself represents the prefix we've built so far. Therefore you don't need to reconstruct the entire prefix just to check whether it exists." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. The DFS transition", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose we're at (r, c) with TrieNode node.\n\nchar ch = board[r][c];\nnext = node.children[ch]\n\nIf next == null: stop.\nOtherwise:\nmark current cell\nDFS(neighbors, next)\nunmark current cell\n\nThat's the entire engine." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10-11. The \"undo\" is critical", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose A -> B -> C. You mark A visited, B visited, C visited. When C's exploration finishes, C = unvisited. Then B's other branch can use C again.\n\nSo: mark -> recurse -> unmark. Without the undo, you're accidentally sharing state between different search paths -- one of the most common backtracking bugs.\n\nExample: grid A B / C D. Path A->B->D marks A,B,D visited. After returning, D and B become unvisited again, so another branch can explore A->C->D.",
        conclusion: "That's why backtracking works." },
      { type: "text", heading: "12. Finding a complete word",
        body: "Trie node may contain boolean isWord. When next.isWord == true, you found a dictionary word. Example: o -> a -> t -> h, Trie says isWord = true, so \"oath\" is an answer." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13. Avoiding duplicate results", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose the same word can be formed through multiple paths -- you may discover \"eat\" multiple times, but the result should contain it once.\n\nOne elegant approach: instead of boolean isWord + String word, store just String word in the terminal Trie node. When found: if (node.word != null) { result.add(node.word); node.word = null; }",
        conclusion: "Now the same dictionary word won't be added again. A very useful interview optimization." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Why storing the word in Trie helps",
        body: "Without storing the complete word, you may need to reconstruct the string from a StringBuilder path. With node.word, you can simply result.add(node.word). So Trie nodes can store word at terminal nodes -- this simplifies the implementation." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15-16. Trie pruning beyond prefix validation — Dynamic Trie pruning", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose after finding a word, its Trie branch has no remaining useful words. You can remove/prune that Trie branch: node.children[c] = null if child has no children AND child.word == null.\n\nExample: dictionary cat, car. You find \"cat\", but ca -> r is still needed for \"car\", so you cannot remove \"ca\" yet. After finding both cat and car, the subtree has no remaining useful words -- then it can be removed.",
        conclusion: "Then future DFS calls won't explore it. This makes future searches cheaper -- very powerful." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17. General principle", tone: "gold",
        body: "A much bigger idea than Trie. In backtracking: if a branch can never produce a new answer, remove it.",
        conclusion: "This is: search-space pruning. You'll see this everywhere." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "18. Backtracking pruning types", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Constraint pruning", note: "Violates constraint -> stop." },
        { id: "2", title: "Bound pruning", note: "Cannot beat current best -> stop." },
        { id: "3", title: "Prefix pruning", note: "Prefix doesn't exist -> stop." },
        { id: "4", title: "State pruning", note: "Same state already solved -> stop." },
        { id: "5", title: "Duplicate pruning", note: "Same result already explored -> skip." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. Trie + Backtracking is really a constraint problem", tone: "gold",
        body: "Think about: current path = \"oat\". Trie says: oat -> valid prefix. So: continue. If current path = \"oax\", Trie says: no such prefix. Therefore: reject.",
        conclusion: "So the Trie is functioning as a constraint oracle. A powerful way to think about it." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20. Pattern A — Word Search II",
        body: "The flagship problem. Recognition: many words + grid + find words -> Trie + Backtracking." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "21-22. Pattern B — Word Squares", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose you need to build ball/area/lead/lady into a square where columns equal rows. At every step you build a prefix. After choosing ball, area, the next row must begin with a specific required prefix (\"le\").\n\nInstead of trying every dictionary word, ask Trie: words starting with \"le\". Then backtrack among only those candidates.",
        conclusion: "So: required prefix -> Trie -> candidate words -> backtracking. A beautiful example of Trie constraining backtracking choices." },
      { type: "text", heading: "23. Pattern C — Crossword / Word Placement",
        body: "Suppose you need to place words into a board under constraints -- empty slots + candidate words + prefix constraints. Trie can reduce candidate selection, then Backtracking tries placements. Less common but the underlying pattern is the same." },
      { type: "text", heading: "24. Pattern D — Generate Valid Strings",
        body: "Suppose: generate all strings of length N that satisfy a dictionary/prefix constraint. You can: Trie -> valid next character -> DFS. Again: Trie = allowed transitions, DFS = enumerate possibilities." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25. Pattern E — Phone T9 / Word Generation",
        body: "Suppose 2->abc, 3->def. Input \"23\". Possible strings: ad, ae, af, bd, be, bf, cd, ce, cf. Classic backtracking. If you additionally restrict results to dictionary words: Trie + Backtracking can prune invalid prefixes." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "26. The important generalization", tone: "gold",
        body: "You don't want to memorize: Word Search II = Trie + Backtracking.",
        conclusion: "You want: many candidate strings + prefix constraints + enumerate possibilities -> Trie + Backtracking. That generalizes." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "27. Trie + Backtracking vs Trie + DFS", items: [
        { label: "Trie + DFS", body: "Mostly: explore the Trie structure itself. Example: autocomplete." },
        { label: "Trie + Backtracking", body: "Usually: explore an external search space (e.g. a grid) while simultaneously following the Trie. Example: grid -> Trie." }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is useful." },
      { type: "text", heading: "28. Trie + Backtracking state",
        body: "A typical state looks like: (gridRow, gridCol, trieNode, visited) or (position, trieNode, currentPath). This is the key abstraction." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "29. When NOT to use Trie + Backtracking", tone: "red",
        body: "Only one word? Just use DFS/backtracking, no need for Trie. Exact dictionary lookup? HashSet may be enough. Prefix isn't relevant? Trie provides little benefit. Simple permutations or subsets? Use Backtracking without Trie." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "30. Backtracking vs DP", items: [
        { label: "Backtracking", body: "Question: what are all possible solutions? You explore branches -- choice -> choice -> choice. Potentially exponential." },
        { label: "DP", body: "Question: are many branches solving the same state? If yes: memoize state." }
      ], conclusion: "Backtracking -> explore possibilities. DP -> reuse repeated states. This distinction becomes very important as you move into DP." },
      { type: "text", heading: "31. Backtracking + Memoization",
        body: "Sometimes a backtracking problem has repeated states. Then DFS(state) can become memo[state]. This converts exponential repeated work into something much more manageable. This is the bridge from Backtracking -> DP. Very important for your preparation." },
      { type: "text", heading: "32. Trie + Backtracking + Memoization",
        body: "Advanced combination: TrieNode + position can define a state. For example: memo[position][trieNode]. Then: if already solved, return memo. Useful when the same Trie state can be reached repeatedly. Don't prioritize this yet, but recognize the combination." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "33. Interview complexity discussion", tone: "gold",
        body: "If asked: what's the complexity of Word Search II? Don't simply say O(N) -- that's incorrect. Backtracking can be exponential.\n\nFor a grid with 4-direction movement and max word length L, a rough search bound per starting cell is O(4 x 3^(L-1)), because after the first cell you generally have at most 3 choices without revisiting the previous cell. With R x C cells: O(R*C*4*3^(L-1)) in a worst-case-style bound, plus Trie construction/output costs.",
        conclusion: "The Trie can dramatically reduce the practical search space through prefix pruning." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "34. Interview answer — strong version",
        intro: "If asked: \"how would you solve Word Search II efficiently?\"",
        items: [
          "\"I'll build a Trie from all dictionary words so common prefixes are shared. Then I'll run DFS/backtracking from every grid cell while carrying the current Trie node. At each step, if the current character isn't a child of the Trie node, I prune immediately. If the Trie node represents a complete word, I add it to the result and clear the stored word to avoid duplicates. I mark cells as visited during recursion and restore them afterward. Optionally, I can prune exhausted Trie branches after their words have been found.\""
        ],
        note: "That's a very strong answer." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "35. Muscle-memory card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see: many dictionary words + search space + strings/prefixes + enumerate valid solutions -- think: Trie + Backtracking.\n\nThen execute:\n1. Build Trie\n2. Start search\n3. Get next choice\n4. Follow Trie\n5. Trie child missing? -> PRUNE\n6. Continue DFS\n7. Record word\n8. Undo state\n\nThe most important line: Trie validates -> Backtracking explores." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 5 progress",
        items: [{ label: "37. Basic Trie", done: true }, { label: "38. Trie + Prefix Search", done: true },
          { label: "39. Trie + DFS", done: true }, { label: "40. Trie + Backtracking", done: true },
          { label: "41. Trie + Wildcard", done: false }, { label: "42. Trie + Frequency", done: false },
          { label: "43. Bitwise Trie", done: false }, { label: "44. Basic Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "45. Subsets", done: false }, { label: "46. Permutations", done: false },
          { label: "47. Combinations", done: false }, { label: "48. Combination Sum", done: false },
          { label: "49. Partitioning", done: false }, { label: "50. Grid Backtracking", done: false },
          { label: "51. Constraint Backtracking", done: false }, { label: "52. Backtracking + Trie", done: false },
          { label: "53. Backtracking + Bitmask", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Trie + Wildcard Search",
        points: ["Teaches an extremely important idea: normal character -> one deterministic Trie branch, wildcard -> multiple possible branches -> DFS/Backtracking", "A reusable pattern beyond Tries: when a problem has a wildcard/unknown choice, expect branching + DFS/backtracking"] }
    ]
  },
};
